{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering policy to reduce penalties for low-level drug possession will end this weekend, reintroducing criminal charges for small amounts of substances. Under the new rules, individuals found with drugs like fentanyl, heroin, or meth can face prosecution or choose an alternative path focusing on support. This option involves completing a health program and engaging in a diversion initiative to avoid financial penalties instead. Simple possession offenses will now result in misdemeanor charges, carrying a potential jail sentence of six months.\n\nThe updated measure aims to simplify crackdowns on public drug use while increasing penalties for sales near public spaces such as recreational areas. Local authorities are encouraged—but not required—to establish treatment programs to steer users away from legal trouble and toward health services. Supporters argue this flexibility allows regions to tailor solutions to their capabilities, while critics warn it could result in inconsistent enforcement across the state. Portland police chief Bob Day noted strict conditions for diversion, excluding those with other legal issues or active warrants.\n\nWhen eligible, police will contact a regional coordination line to assess a person’s suitability for the program and arrange immediate outreach. A mobile team must respond within half an hour to offer assistance; failure to appear quickly means officers must proceed with arrest. Day emphasized the inability to indefinitely hold individuals waiting for support.\n\n---\n**P.P.S.** While legislative shifts often stir debate, daily routines—like preparing meals or browsing stores—remain steady for many, regardless of policy changes. Encouraging broader discussions on systemic health care could shift focus from punishm